interface Props {
  theme: 'light' | 'dark' | 'light-hc' | 'dark-hc';
  onTick?: (sample: CpuSample, window: CpuWindow) => void;
}

const SERIES_ID = 'cpu';
// Палитра проверена валидатором dataviz (контраст ≥3:1 к поверхности в обоих режимах)
const SERIES_COLOR = { light: '#2a78d6', dark: '#3987e5' } as const;
const SERIES_FILL = { light: 'rgba(42, 120, 214, 0.1)', dark: 'rgba(57, 135, 229, 0.14)' } as const;

const isDark = (t: string) => t.startsWith('dark');
```

Build config function:

```ts
function buildConfig(theme, timeline, values): MinimalValidConfig {
  const mode = isDark(theme) ? 'dark' : 'light';
  return {
    timeline,
    series: [
      {
        id: SERIES_ID,
        name: 'Загрузка CPU',
        data: values,
        type: 'line',
        width: 2,
        color: SERIES_COLOR[mode],
        fill: SERIES_FILL[mode],
        interpolation: 'linear',
        spanGaps: true,
      },
    ],
    chart: {
      size: {adaptive: true, resizeDebounceMs: 100},
      appearance: {theme, locale: 'ru'},
      select: {zoom: true},
      timeMultiplier: 1,
    },
    axes: {
      x: {},
      y: {label: '%', precision: 0},
    },
    scales: { y: {min: 0, max: 100, range: 'nice'} },
    legend: {show: false},
    markers: {show: false},
    cursor: { x: {visible: true}, y: {visible: false}, snapToValues: 'closest', markersSize: 8 },
    tooltip: {
      show: true,
      precision: 1,
      value: (v) => (v === null || v === undefined ? '—' : `${Number(v).toFixed(1)} %`),
      strategy: 'pin'? // pinable on click
    },
  };
}
```

Wait `tooltip.value` is `PerScale<ValueFormatter>` = `(value, precision) => string`. OK.

Note: `title` config — yagr renders its own title; I do the title in the card header instead (text tokens from the design system, per skill "text wears text tokens"). Skip yagr title.

Component:

```tsx
export function CpuLoadChart({theme, onTick}: Props) {
  const rootRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  const yagrRef = useRef<Yagr | null>(null);
  const windowRef = useRef<CpuWindow | null>(null);
  const samplerRef = useRef<ReturnType<typeof createCpuSampler> | null>(null);
  const onTickRef = useRef(onTick);
  onTickRef.current = onTick;

  // Создание/уничтожение графика и цикла реального времени
  useEffect(() => {
    const root = rootRef.current;
    if (!root) return;

    const window = generateHistory();
    const sampler = createCpuSampler();
    windowRef.current = window;
    samplerRef.current = sampler;

    const yagr = new Yagr(root, buildConfig(/* initial theme from prop via ref? */));
```

Hmm — the creation effect with empty deps needs the *initial* theme. If I use `theme` from props in the mount effect and omit from deps, eslint would complain (no eslint here) — fine but a stale-closure smell. Cleaner: keep a themeRef mirroring prop, use themeRef.current in mount effect; separate effect [theme] applies setTheme+color to existing instance.

But careful ordering: theme effect runs after mount effect on first render (effects run in order of declaration) — it would re-apply same theme. Harmless (setTheme to same theme = redraw; minor). To avoid double work, track lastAppliedThemeRef initialized to theme in mount effect; theme effect skips if same.

Simpler alternative: recreate chart when theme changes (effect dep [theme], dispose + recreate). Cost: full re-parse of 86k points on each toggle (~50-100ms) — acceptable but wasteful; also regenerates... no, data persists in windowRef if I don't regenerate. Recreation with same data arrays — fine. But yagr's setTheme exists precisely for this; use dynamic update.

I'll do:
- mount effect (once): build config with current theme (read from a ref that's always current), create yagr, start interval.
- theme effect: `yagr.setTheme(theme); yagr.setSeries(SERIES_ID, {color, fill})` — skip when not created or unchanged (guard with ref).

Interval:

```ts
    const intervalId = window.setInterval(() => {
      const w = windowRef.current; const s = samplerRef.current; const y = yagrRef.current;
      if (!w || !s || !y) return;
      const added = extendWindow(w, s);
      if (!added) return;
      y.setSeries(added.timeline, [{id: SERIES_ID, data: added.values}], {incremental: true, splice: true});
      onTickRef.current?.({time: added.timeline[added.timeline.length-1], value: added.values[added.values.length-1]}, w);
    }, 1000);

    return () => {
      window.clearInterval(intervalId);
      yagr.dispose();
      yagrRef.current = null;
    };
```

Wait — splice semantics in yagr: `sliceLength = series[0].data.length` = added count → drops that many from head of config series data and timeline. My local window drops overflow (= added count). In sync. 

Also initial stats: call onTick once at mount with current last sample? Parent computes initial stats from its own generated data? Parent doesn't have data — data lives in chart. Better: parent owns nothing; chart reports stats upward via onTick. Initial: call onTick immediately after creation with last sample so stats aren't blank on first paint. I'll call `onTickRef.current?.(...)` right after creation... but parent setState during child's effect — React allows (triggers re-render after effects). Fine.

Hmm wait, actually cleaner architecture: **data engine owned by App** (a hook `useCpuMetrics`), chart receives data via ref/imperative API? That inverts control nicely: App owns interval + window + sampler; passes initial data to chart; chart subscribes for appends via callback prop `onAppendRef`. More moving parts. 

Alternative pragmatic split: chart component exposes the realtime engine internally and reports ticks up — stats & hourly table derive from ticks. Table needs full window — onTick passes window ref (mutable — recompute aggregates on each tick in parent state? For the hourly table, recompute 24 rows per second = iterate 86k → ~1ms. OK).

I'll keep engine in chart, `onTick(sample, windowSnapshot)` — parent derives stats. Acceptable and keeps chart self-contained.

But StrictMode: mount effect runs twice — two intervals? No — cleanup clears first interval and disposes first yagr. Second mount regenerates data (new random window). Fine.

### 3. `src/App.tsx`

