events.content:
Hover works: crosshair vertical line, marker dot on the line, tooltip "● Загрузка CPU 37.6 %" with series color key ✓.

Zoom: hmm, the "zoomed" screenshot still shows the full 24h range (20:00–18:00). The drag didn't zoom? In the zoomed.png the range looks unchanged. Wait — the mouse.down/mouse.move/mouse.up sequence: uPlot zoom requires drag on the plot area; my coordinates were within the canvas (60% height). Maybe the drag was too fast or selection needs to start over the over-area... The screenshot after drag shows same range. Possibly yagr's select.zoom works but my synthetic mouse events didn't trigger uPlot's mousedown handler (needs pointer events? uPlot uses mouse events — should work). OR the drag happened but then... after mouse.up, uPlot sets scale to selection — the screenshot shows unchanged labels 20:00..18:00 — no zoom.

Wait, look again at zoomed.png: it shows full range. So zoom didn't happen. Also interesting: there's a red vertical line at 08:00 and marker — that's the pinned tooltip? I clicked (down+up at different points = drag, not click) — the tooltip moved. The red line is yagr's selection? In zoomed.png there's a vertical line at ~08:00 with marker — could be leftover hover.

Why no zoom? Maybe uPlot requires the drag on the "over" element which is fine... Or yagr's `chart.select.zoom` option — default true — maybe the selection started but minWidth not met? Drag from 30% to 50% = 20% of ~1100px = 220px, way above min. 

Hmm, maybe playwright's mouse.move with steps after down did trigger, but uPlot zoom needs `drag: {x: true}` — default in uPlot is drag x true. Yagr sets select zoom by default true.

Wait — maybe the zoom DID occur but realtime update (1s tick) reset the scale! My interval calls yagr.setSeries(...) → batch.recalc → uplot.setData — uPlot's setData by default resets scales to data range! Yes — `u.setData(data)` triggers `_setScale` auto-ranging to full data. So after each 1s tick, any user zoom is wiped out. That's a real UX bug for the "interactive" requirement: user zooms in, one second later the view resets.

How do realtime uPlot dashboards handle it? Keep x scale fixed on update: preserve current scale range: before setData, capture `u.scales.x.min/max` and set `scale: {x: {min, max}}` — or use `u.setData(data, false)` (resetScales=false). uPlot's setData signature: setData(data, resetScales = true). Yagr's batch.recalc calls `this.uplot.setData(this.series)` — resetScales defaults true → zoom reset every second. 

Options:
1. After zoom (or any scale change), remember user's x range and restore after each update via `uplot.setScale('x', {min, max})` — but while zoomed into the past, new points still append; the view should stay on the selected window (not follow). Listen to uPlot's setSelect / scale changes: yagr hooks `scaleUpdate`? There's `hooks: {scaleUpdate: [...]}` in YagrHooks. Also uPlot hooks via `hooks` (YagrHooks = Hooks.Arrays & InternalYargHooks) — I can pass `hooks: {setScale: [(u) => ...]}`? uPlot hooks include 'setScale'. I can capture user zoom there.

2. Simpler robust: track "user zoomed" state: on uPlot 'setSelect' hook (fired on drag select before zoom) or on dblclick reset. When user selects a range → store range; after each data update, if stored range, apply `uplot.setScale('x', range)`. On dblclick, uPlot resets scale to data range → 'setScale' hook fires with min/max = full data → treat as "unzoomed" (detect: range ≈ full data range).

Yagr config `hooks` accepts uPlot hook arrays (Hooks.Arrays). Let me implement:

```ts
hooks: {
  setScale: [
    (u: uPlot, key: string) => {
      if (key !== 'x') return;
      // запоминаем текущий диапазон после действий пользователя
    },
  ],
}
```

But setScale also fires on every data update (auto-range). Distinguishing user-zoom from auto-reset is tricky. Approach: after each data update, restore user range if present:

- Maintain `zoomRef: {min, max} | null`.
- On uPlot 'setSelect' event? uPlot fires 'setSelect' hook on drag end (before zoom). Actually uPlot hook 'setSelect' fires when selection changes (during drag and on mouseup). On mouseup, uPlot zooms: setScale('x', {min: selMin, max: selMax}).
- Use yagr's `hooks.onSelect` (yagr's own): `onSelect?: HookHandler<{from, to}>` — yagr fires this on range selection (used in react wrapper onSelect prop). Set zoomRef = {min: from, max: to} there (converted units? from/to in data units = ms presumably).
- After each tick's setSeries, if zoomRef, call `yagr.uplot.setScale('x', zoomRef)` (uPlot setScale takes scale values — seconds or whatever uPlot uses internally... uPlot x in seconds? Since splits came in ms, uPlot data x is in ms?? My earlier analysis: splits were in ms → uPlot works in ms units. setScale would take ms then. The onSelect from/to — in the react wrapper signature `onSelect?: (from: number, to: number)` — likely ms as well.)
- On dblclick reset: uPlot resets to full range; clear zoomRef. How to detect? uPlot fires 'setScale' with the auto range. Yagr hook scaleUpdate? Simplest: after dblclick, uPlot sets scale to data min/max — I can detect in uPlot's 'setScale' hook: if the new range covers ~full data span, clear zoomRef. Hmm, but setScale also fires from my restore call (guarded: set a flag when applying restore).

Plan:
```ts
const applyingRef = {current: false};
hooks: {
  setScale: [
    (u, key) => {
      if (key !== 'x' || applyingRef.current) return;
      const {min, max} = u.scales.x;
      // если диапазон совпадает с полным — пользователь сбросил зум (dblclick)
      const dataMin = u.data[0][0]; const dataMax = u.data[0][u.data[0].length-1];
      if (min <= dataMin && max >= dataMax) zoomRef.current = null;
      else zoomRef.current = {min, max};
    },
  ],
}
```
Wait but setScale fires during every setData auto-range too — with full range → clears zoomRef. Order: setSeries → uplot.setData → auto setScale(full) → hook fires → zoomRef cleared → my restore after setSeries has nothing to restore. Need to capture BEFORE setData.

Better: in the tick handler, before calling yagr.setSeries, read `const cur = yagr.uplot.scales.x; const full = data range;` if cur range is narrower than full → user zoomed → after setSeries, re-apply `{min: cur.min, max: cur.max}` via setScale. No hooks needed! The zoomRef persistence: each tick reads the current scale — if user is zoomed, current scale stays the zoomed range (since I restore it every tick). If user dblclicks → uPlot resets to full → next tick sees full range → no restore. Clean and hookless.

Edge: at full range (no zoom), no restore needed; the chart follows the sliding window (nice — live tail).

One nuance: while zoomed, new points append but view stays — good. Also when zoomed, min/max in ms — uPlot setScale('x', {min, max}) uses data units (ms here).

Also careful: `u.scales.x.min/max` may be null initially. Guard.

Implement in the interval:
```ts
const u = yagr.uplot;
const xr = u.scales.x;
const zoomed = xr.min != null && xr.max != null && (xr.min > addedFirstTimelineBound || xr.max < lastT);
```
Simpler: compare against full data range BEFORE update: dataMin = w.timeline[0], dataMax = w.timeline[last]. If xr.min > dataMin + epsilon || xr.max < dataMax - epsilon → zoomed.

After setSeries, if zoomed → `u.setScale('x', {min: savedMin, max: savedMax})`.

Also on window slide, zoomed range stays fixed in absolute time — eventually slides out of view as window moves; the data range moves forward but zoomed view stays — user sees static historical window until dblclick. Acceptable and standard.

Also: while zoomed, auto-ranging of Y might jump as new spikes arrive — uPlot setData auto-ranges y too. Restore y as well? Y is fixed 0–100 by scales config (min: 0, max: 100) — yagr scale min/max fixed → y doesn't auto-range. ✓

